KEYPROBE(1)

NAME
    keyprobe - check that netd refuses an ordinary user's key operations

SYNOPSIS
    keyprobe

DESCRIPTION
    netd holds the cluster keys of the users logged in on this machine
    (login hands each one over and logout drops it). Every program can
    send to netd, so the table is only as safe as netd's own checks.
    keyprobe tries each key operation as the user running it and prints
    one [ok] or [FAIL] line per check:

      a hold is refused        (only root may give netd a key)
      a list is refused        (only root may see which users are held)
      a drop of each handle is refused or finds nothing
                               (only the holder or root may drop a key)
      a drop of "mine" drops nothing

    It refuses to run as root, where every one of these may succeed and a
    drop would destroy real state.

EXIT STATUS
    The number of failed checks; 2 when run as root.

SEE ALSO
    clusterkey(1) (clusterkey held lists the table, as root), login(1)
